1. PURPOSE AND AUTHORITY

This constitution establishes the mandatory rules for planning, building, testing, publishing, operating, securing, maintaining and repairing the Mwangaza Hardware website.

Its objectives are to:

1. Minimize website freezing, lag, broken pages, failed forms, data loss and unexpected downtime.
2. Make routine website management possible through ChatGPT, GitHub and the project owner without routinely hiring a developer.
3. Require evidence before declaring a feature, correction or release successful.
4. Protect customers, business records, payments, privacy and the interests of Mwangaza Hardware.
5. Keep costs under control and avoid unnecessary paid services.
6. Make future improvements safe, reversible and understandable.
7. Prevent unapproved changes to prices, policies, commissions, payment destinations or business claims.
8. Maintain a documented way to diagnose and recover from failures.

Authority rule: This constitution is the governing project specification. Other documents, AI instructions, code comments and individual requests must not silently contradict it.

If a conflict arises, stop the affected change, record the conflict and obtain the appropriate approval before proceeding.

This constitution does not override applicable law, platform requirements, payment-provider requirements or security obligations.

2. THE THREE-PART WORKING SYSTEM

The project will operate through three primary resources.

2.1 ChatGPT

ChatGPT will assist with:

- Translating requirements into clear, small tasks.
- Explaining code and configuration in understandable language.
- Writing and reviewing code.
- Diagnosing errors from logs, screenshots and test results.
- Preparing GitHub-ready changes and repair instructions.
- Designing automated tests.
- Reviewing security, performance, accessibility and usability.
- Explaining deployment failures and recovery options.
- Preparing content, catalogue structures, learning materials and marketing plans.
- Maintaining consistency with this constitution.

ChatGPT must distinguish between a proposed fix, a fix that has been committed, a fix that has passed tests and a fix that has been verified on the live website.

ChatGPT must never claim to have inspected, changed, tested, deployed or restored something unless the relevant action and evidence confirm it.

2.2 GitHub

GitHub will hold the version-controlled source of truth for:

- Website source code.
- Product and architecture specifications.
- Project decisions and approved requirements.
- Automated tests and workflows.
- AI coding instructions.
- Security and release checklists.
- Incident and recovery instructions.
- Change history and known-good versions.

Secrets, passwords, private keys, customer information and payment credentials must not be stored in public source files.

GitHub is not, by itself, a backup of every external service, database, uploaded image or payment-provider record.

2.3 Project owner

The project owner will:

- Confirm business facts, products, prices, stock, policies and authorized contacts.
- Review proposed changes and approve sensitive business decisions.
- Follow the agreed testing and publication process.
- Provide screenshots, error messages and other evidence when needed.
- Keep account access and recovery methods secure.
- Approve spending and paid integrations.
- Confirm when a release may proceed to production.
- Keep Mwangaza Hardware and marketing-agency finances appropriately separated.

The owner must never be asked to paste passwords, one-time codes, private API keys or card details into a public repository or ordinary chat.

3. NON-NEGOTIABLE PROJECT RULES

1. No feature is considered complete merely because its code has been written.
2. No production release is approved while a critical security issue or unresolved release-blocking test failure remains.
3. No untested change may be described as safe for production.
4. No live payment integration is enabled before merchant ownership, settlement, fees, security and reconciliation have been verified.
5. No customer payment is directed to the agency's personal account.
6. No prices, stock status, discounts, tax rules, delivery fees or commission terms are invented.
7. No customer-facing AI assistant may invent product availability, claim a payment succeeded without verification or promise an unapproved delivery date.
8. No change may delete or overwrite important business records without authorization and a recovery plan.
9. No secrets or customer records may be committed to the public repository.
10. No deployment is declared successful solely because a GitHub workflow says that it passed.
11. Every important failure must have a documented diagnostic and recovery route.
12. Routine repairs must favor simple, understandable solutions over unnecessary complexity.
13. A change must not silently remove an existing working feature.
14. Business rules must be tested as well as technical behavior.
15. When evidence is missing, the status must be recorded as unverified.

