5. REQUIREMENTS AND CHANGE CONTROL

Every feature must have:

- A clear purpose.
- Defined inputs and expected outputs.
- Acceptance criteria.
- Relevant tests.
- Known limitations.
- A recovery method appropriate to its risk.
- A record of any business decision it depends on.

Change procedure

1. Identify the problem or requested improvement.
2. Read the relevant project documentation.
3. Determine which modules and business rules could be affected.
4. Record the intended change and its acceptance criteria.
5. Inspect the current implementation before editing.
6. Make the smallest reasonable change.
7. Run the required automated tests.
8. Review the resulting code and test output.
9. Test the affected user journey.
10. Record the outcome and any remaining risks.
11. Merge and deploy only when the applicable release gates pass.

If a test fails, investigate the failure before making further unrelated changes.

Changes to payment destinations, commission rules, personal-data handling, access permissions, production secrets or destructive database operations require explicit authorized approval.

6. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE

The initial website should remain as simple as reasonably possible.

6.1 Architecture principles

- Use a maintained, well-supported framework and documented dependencies.
- Keep the presentation layer separate from business rules.
- Keep sensitive server operations away from public browser code.
- Use a database only when the required functionality justifies it.
- Keep the catalogue and calculator usable without an AI service.
- Make optional integrations replaceable without rebuilding the entire site.
- Avoid unnecessary libraries, background processes and third-party scripts.
- Avoid duplicating product prices or business rules across multiple files.
- Centralize configuration and validate it before deployment.
- Keep development, preview and production environments separate where supported.
- Use documented migrations for database structure changes.
- Never depend on an undocumented manual action to make the website work.

6.2 Graceful degradation

If an optional service fails, the rest of the website should continue working where practical.

Examples:

- If AI support is unavailable, show the FAQ and contact options.
- If WhatsApp automation fails, retain the normal contact link.
- If analytics fails, do not block catalogue browsing.
- If an external image fails, show a sensible fallback.
- If the network drops during a form submission, explain the problem and prevent accidental duplicate submissions.
- If the database is unavailable, do not display invented product data or pretend an enquiry was saved.

7. PERFORMANCE AND STABILITY

Performance is a release requirement, not an optional cosmetic improvement.

7.1 Mandatory controls

- Resize and compress images appropriately.
- Use modern image formats where supported.
- Load nonessential images and content lazily.
- Minimize unnecessary JavaScript, CSS and dependencies.
- Avoid excessive animation and large third-party scripts.
- Prevent layout shifts when images and other content load.
- Avoid repeated network requests and inefficient database queries.
- Paginate or progressively load large catalogues.
- Cache appropriate public resources without exposing private data.
- Display clear loading, empty, success and error states.
- Ensure the website remains usable on common Android devices and mobile browsers.

7.2 Performance targets

Where real-user data is available, aim for the following Core Web Vitals at the 75th percentile:

- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP): 2.5 seconds or less.
- Interaction to Next Paint (INP): 200 milliseconds or less.
- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS): 0.1 or less.

These targets are measured over real user experiences where sufficient data exists. A single laboratory test cannot establish that the targets have been achieved for all visitors.

Before real-user data exists, use repeatable laboratory tests and representative Android-device testing. Record results and investigate regressions.

7.3 Performance release gate

Before release:

- Run an automated build.
- Run applicable performance and accessibility checks.
- Check major pages on a narrow mobile viewport.
- Inspect image loading and page responsiveness.
- Test the calculator with realistic quantities and catalogue sizes.
- Check the browser console and network errors.
- Compare results with the previous known-good version.

A serious regression must be fixed or the release blocked.

8. SECURITY AND PRIVACY

8.1 Access control

- Give each account only the permissions it needs.
- Protect the production branch and sensitive settings.
- Enable multifactor authentication where available.
- Do not share owner credentials with the marketing agency.
- Separate public visitors, staff, agency and owner permissions where applicable.
- Remove access when it is no longer required.
- Use server-side authorization for protected operations.

Hiding a button in the browser is not sufficient access control.

8.2 Secrets

- Never put API keys, passwords, private keys or payment credentials in public code.
- Use the hosting platform's secret-management features or protected environment variables.
- Keep privileged credentials server-side.
- Use separate credentials for test and production environments.
- Rotate exposed credentials immediately.
- Remove a leaked secret from active use even if the offending commit is later deleted.

Deleting a secret from the latest file does not necessarily remove it from Git history or copies.

8.3 Input validation and web security

- Validate all inputs on the server whenever a server operation is involved.
- Escape or safely render user-supplied text.
- Protect against injection, cross-site scripting, request forgery and unauthorized access as applicable.
- Apply sensible rate limits to public forms and automated endpoints.
- Keep dependencies updated and check for known vulnerabilities.
- Use HTTPS for the live website.
- Configure secure cookies, headers and session handling where applicable.
- Avoid exposing detailed internal error traces to visitors.
- Log security-relevant events without unnecessarily recording personal information.

8.4 Privacy

Collect only information needed for a defined purpose.

Before collecting customer details, explain the relevant purpose and provide an appropriate privacy notice. Define retention, access, correction and deletion procedures consistent with applicable law.

Analytics, marketing messages, cookies and automated communications must be configured according to applicable privacy and consent requirements.

Review the Kenyan Data Protection Act and applicable regulations before launching features that process personal information. Obtain qualified advice if the legal position or risk is unclear.

8.5 Security release gate

Do not launch or continue a materially affected feature while a known critical vulnerability remains unresolved.

Security checks must include:

- Dependency and vulnerability scanning.
- Secret scanning where available.
- Authentication and authorization tests.
- Input validation tests.
- Production configuration review.
- Permission and data-access checks.
- Review of any new third-party integration.

Automated scanning reduces risk but does not prove that the website is secure.

9. CATALOGUE AND MATERIALS CALCULATOR

9.1 Catalogue rules

Every product must have a stable identifier, name, category, unit of sale, price and relevant descriptive information.

Stock status, delivery promises, discounts and product specifications must reflect verified business information.

Sample data must be clearly identified as demonstration data and must never be mistaken for a confirmed selling price.

9.2 Calculator formula

For each line:

Line amount = Quantity × Unit price

Subtotal = Sum of all line amounts

Total = Subtotal − Approved discount + Applicable tax + Applicable delivery charge

Each adjustment must be included only when its rules are confirmed and applicable.

9.3 Calculator tests

Test at least:

- Zero, negative and decimal quantities where relevant.
- Empty inputs and invalid characters.
- Very large quantities.
- Products with different units.
- Products with missing or unavailable prices.
- Price changes after an item has been selected.
- Adding, removing and editing items.
- Rounding and currency display.
- Approved discounts and tax rules.
- Delivery charges where applicable.
- Empty estimates.
- Mobile-screen behavior.
- Copying or submitting an estimate.

The calculator must not silently convert invalid input into a misleading total.

9.4 Catalogue consistency

Where practical, the calculator must read the same authoritative price data used by the catalogue.

If a price changes, affected calculations must update or clearly identify that an earlier estimate is based on an older price.

An estimate is not an order confirmation unless the business explicitly defines and implements that process.

10. DIY LEARNING CENTRE

Educational material should be useful, accurate and appropriately cautious.

Topics may include waterproofing, plumbing materials, roofing, insulation, tile adhesives, solar and efficient lighting, water-saving technology and quantity estimation.

Requirements:

- Distinguish general guidance from professional advice.
- Identify safety-critical steps and when a qualified professional is required.
- Do not make unsupported product performance or certification claims.
- Avoid unsafe electrical, structural, chemical or height-related instructions.
- Review technical content before publication.
- Correct inaccurate content promptly.
- Clearly date or version important technical guidance when useful.

The Learning Centre must not become dependent on an AI service to display already-approved educational content.

11. CUSTOMER SUPPORT AND AUTOMATION

11.1 Support behavior

The support assistant must:

- Use approved product and policy information.
- Explain how to use the calculator.
