16. MANDATORY WEBSITE RELEASE GATES

A release may proceed only when every applicable gate is passed or an explicitly authorized, documented exception is recorded.

Gate 1: Requirements

- The change has an approved purpose.
- Acceptance criteria are defined.
- Affected features and business rules are identified.

Gate 2: Code quality

- Changes are understandable and reviewable.
- No unrelated working functionality has been removed.
- Required linting, type checks and builds pass.

Gate 3: Automated tests

- Required unit, integration and end-to-end tests pass.
- The relevant regression tests pass.
- Failures have been investigated rather than ignored.

Gate 4: Security

- No known unresolved critical vulnerability affects the release.
- No secrets have been exposed.
- Access controls and production configuration have been reviewed.
- Sensitive operations are protected server-side.

Gate 5: Performance

- Major pages have been tested.
- No unacceptable performance regression is known.
- Images and dependencies are reasonably optimized.
- Mobile behavior has been checked.

Gate 6: Usability and accessibility

- Navigation, forms, labels, contrast and error states have been checked.
- The relevant user journey works on a mobile device.
- Keyboard and assistive-technology behavior is tested where applicable.

Gate 7: Business accuracy

- Prices, product details, contacts and policies are approved.
- Demonstration data cannot be mistaken for live data.
- No unapproved commission or payment rule has been introduced.

Gate 8: Data and recovery

- Relevant data has been backed up where applicable.
- Database changes have a documented migration and recovery plan.
- The previous known-good version or an appropriate rollback procedure is available.
- Destructive operations have been reviewed and authorized.

Gate 9: Deployment

- The production build succeeds.
- Required environment settings are present.
- The deployed version corresponds to the approved source revision.
- The deployment provider reports success.

Gate 10: Live verification

- The live home page and key routes open.
- The affected feature works on a real device.
- Forms, links and important calculations work.
- Browser errors and relevant service logs have been checked.
- No false success messages are shown.

Gate 11: Monitoring

- Monitoring and error reporting are enabled where supported.
- A way to detect and investigate failures exists.
- The responsible person knows where to find logs and deployment history.

Gate 12: Sign-off

- The evidence and remaining limitations are recorded.
- The authorized release decision is documented.
- Any exception has an owner, reason and expiry or follow-up date.

A checklist marked complete without evidence is not a passed gate.

Payment, authentication, database and commission releases require additional tests specific to those functions.

17. SELF-SERVICE DIAGNOSTICS AND REPAIR

The default repair process is:

1. Stop: Do not make multiple speculative edits.
2. Describe: Record what is broken, when it started and who is affected.
3. Capture: Obtain the exact error message, screenshot, workflow log or relevant URL.
4. Locate: Identify the last change and affected component.
5. Compare: Check the previous known-good version and recent deployment history.
6. Diagnose: Use ChatGPT to interpret the evidence and propose a testable cause.
7. Back up: Preserve affected data or configuration before a risky repair.
8. Change: Make one small correction.
9. Test: Run the failed test plus relevant regression tests.
10. Verify: Test the repaired behavior in preview or staging, then production where appropriate.
11. Record: Document the cause, correction and outcome.
12. Escalate safely: If evidence is insufficient or the repair risks data, money or security, stop and obtain the necessary authorized help.

Never make random production edits to see whether something improves.

17.1 Repair classifications

Class A: Routine and low risk
Examples: incorrect text, a broken internal link, a spacing problem or a small layout defect.

Approach: diagnose, make a small change, run checks and verify on mobile.

Class B: Functional or integration problem
Examples: calculator defects, form failures, failed API requests or deployment errors.

Approach: inspect logs and inputs, reproduce the failure, correct the cause, add a regression test and verify the full user journey.

Class C: High-risk incident
Examples: exposed credentials, unauthorized access, corrupted financial data, incorrect payment settlement or suspected personal-data breach.

Approach: contain the problem, preserve evidence, protect accounts and data, notify the authorized owner, follow applicable reporting requirements and restore only through a verified procedure. Do not attempt speculative fixes.

17.2 What self-service means

The project should be designed so routine code changes, configuration corrections, content updates, test runs, deployments and rollbacks can be performed through ChatGPT-assisted GitHub workflows and documented hosting controls.

It does not guarantee that every incident can be solved without a specialist. Hosting outages, payment-provider failures, compromised accounts, complex data corruption and legal or regulatory questions may require the relevant provider or qualified professional.

The objective is to minimize such dependency while recognizing its limits honestly.

18. BACKUP, RESTORE AND ROLLBACK

Maintain separate recovery arrangements for:

- Source code.
- Database records.
- Product images and uploaded documents.
- Hosting and DNS configuration.
- Required environment configuration.
- Payment and reconciliation records held by authorized providers.

Never place secret values in backups stored in public repositories.

Required procedures

- Define which data must be backed up and how often.
- Define a retention period appropriate to the data.
- Protect backup access.
- Document restoration steps.
- Test restoration in a safe environment.
- Record the last successful restore test.
- Identify the last known-good application release.
- Document how to reverse a failed deployment.
- Ensure a rollback does not destroy newer legitimate orders or financial records.

A backup is not considered reliable merely because a backup file exists. A restoration test must demonstrate that it can be recovered.

19. INCIDENT RESPONSE

For an outage or serious failure:

Step 1: Record the affected page, start time, error and impact.

Step 2: Check hosting status, deployment history, logs and relevant external-service status.

Step 3: If the failure began after a deployment, assess whether a verified rollback is safer than a new code change.

Step 4: For security incidents, revoke or rotate compromised credentials, restrict affected access and preserve evidence.

Step 5: For payment or financial discrepancies, stop affected automated financial actions and reconcile against trusted provider records.

Step 6: Restore service using a verified fix or recovery process.

Step 7: Test the repaired user journey and monitor it.

Step 8: Record the root cause, corrective action and a preventive test or control.

If a personal-data breach or financial incident occurs, follow the applicable legal, contractual and provider notification obligations. Do not delay required reporting while attempting to solve the technical issue.

20. COST CONTROL

Every external service must have a defined purpose and a cost assessment.

Before enabling a paid service, document:

- Setup fee.
- Monthly or annual fee.
- Usage-based charges.
- Payment-processing fees.
- Expected volume and cost range.
- Spending limits and alerts where available.
- Cancellation procedure.
- Free or lower-cost alternative.
- Data export and exit options.

Avoid unnecessary paid plugins, duplicate analytics systems, excessive AI calls and infrastructure that is not yet justified by actual usage.

The website must retain core browsing and calculator functionality when optional paid services are unavailable.

No service may be represented as free or within budget without checking its applicable terms and usage charges.

21. DOCUMENTATION AND LEARNING

For each important technical change, record:

- What changed.
- Why it changed.
- Which files or settings were affected.
- How it was tested.
- Whether it was deployed.
- Any remaining risks.
- How to reverse the change where appropriate.

Instructions must be understandable to a non-developer using an Android phone and GitHub.

Where a task requires copying code, specify the exact file and location. Where it requires changing a setting, identify the setting and its expected value. Ask for a screenshot or test result before proceeding when verification is necessary.

Do not give a long sequence of dependent instructions when the next step should first be verified.

22. ACCESSIBILITY AND BROWSER COMPATIBILITY

The website must:

- Use readable text and adequate contrast.
- Provide meaningful labels for form controls.
- Display clear validation and error messages.
- Support keyboard navigation where applicable.
- Provide useful alternative text for meaningful images.
- Avoid relying on color alone to communicate errors.
- Use adequately sized mobile interaction targets.
- Remain usable when optional animations or scripts fail.
- Be tested in common Android browsers and relevant viewport sizes.

Accessibility checks must be automated where possible and supplemented by manual testing.

23. LEGAL AND BUSINESS APPROVAL

Before the public launch, confirm the applicable requirements for:

- Privacy notice and personal-data processing.
- Terms of use.
- Customer enquiries and marketing consent.
- Consumer information and advertising claims.
- Refunds, cancellations and delivery terms if orders are introduced.
- Tax and invoice treatment where applicable.
- Product claims and intellectual property.
- Agency compensation and commission agreements.
- Payment handling and financial records.

Only verified company information may appear as fact on the website.

The business owner must approve the use of company branding, official contacts, product prices, stock claims, policies and payment arrangements.

24. PROJECT PHASES

The implementation order is:

Phase 0 — Governance
Adopt this constitution, establish repository documents, confirm account ownership and configure the safest available GitHub protections.

Phase 1 — Website foundation
Choose the stack, establish the project structure, create a basic build and deployment workflow, and verify a working preview.

Phase 2 — Public website
Build responsive navigation, Home, About, Help and Contact pages.

Phase 3 — Product catalogue
Add product categories, search, units, approved prices and clearly labelled sample data where required.

Phase 4 — Materials calculator
Implement the formula, input validation, price linkage and automated tests.

Phase 5 — DIY Learning Centre
Add reviewed educational content and safe guidance.

Phase 6 — Enquiries and support
Add validated enquiry forms, basic scripted support, contact links and safe failure handling.

Phase 7 — Marketing measurement
Add proportionate analytics, campaign attribution and reporting after privacy and consent requirements are reviewed.

Phase 8 — Reliability hardening
Improve performance, security, accessibility, monitoring, backups and incident recovery based on evidence.

Phase 9 — Payment and commission preparation
Confirm written business terms, select a verified provider, design protected financial records and test the entire process in a non-production environment.

Phase 10 — Controlled production launch
Pass all applicable release gates, verify the live website and monitor for regressions.

No phase is considered complete merely because its estimated time has elapsed.

25. ACCEPTANCE AND AMENDMENTS

This constitution becomes the project's governing standard when the authorized project owner approves it and the approved version is committed to GitHub.

Material amendments must:

1. Identify the proposed change.
2. Explain why it is needed.
3. Identify the affected requirements.
4. Assess security, financial, privacy and reliability consequences.
5. Update relevant tests and documentation.
6. Receive the required approval.
7. Be recorded in the project decision history.

A change must not silently weaken a release gate or remove an established security control.

Emergency exceptions must be documented, limited in scope, approved by the appropriate person and reviewed after the incident.

26. DEFINITION OF DONE

A feature is done only when:

- It satisfies its approved requirements.
- The code is committed to the correct branch.
- Applicable automated checks pass.
- Relevant regression tests pass.
- The feature has been manually verified where needed.
- Security and privacy implications have been reviewed.
- Documentation has been updated.
- Recovery or rollback arrangements exist where appropriate.
- Remaining limitations are recorded.

A release is verified in production only when the approved version has been deployed and the relevant live checks have passed.

A project is not guaranteed never to fail. Its reliability is demonstrated by measured performance, passing tests, secure practices, recoverable data, documented incident handling and verified releases.

Final governing principle: We do not rely on memory, assumptions or promises. We rely on documented rules, automated checks, evidence, controlled changes and tested recovery procedures.
